<section id=3D"introduction" class=3D"level1 page-columns page-full">
<h1>Introduction</h1>
<p>Here is one (very negative) view about the current state of Sociology:</=
p>
<div class=3D"page-columns page-full"><blockquote class=3D"blockquote">
<p>According to Ray Rodrigues, chancellor of the state university system, F=
lorida lawmakers began eyeing general education as a target of reform after=
 complaints from parents. Their children would come home for Thanksgiving b=
reak during their first semester in college and would =E2=80=9Cbe communica=
ting cultural Marxism=E2=80=9D=E2=80=A6Under SB 266 [a Florida state law in=
tended to eliminate =E2=80=9Cwoke=E2=80=9D ideology], courses that count to=
ward general-education credit should =E2=80=9Cpromote and preserve the cons=
titutional republic=E2=80=9D through =E2=80=9Ctraditional, historically acc=
urate=E2=80=9D coursework. The law also says that =E2=80=9Ccore courses=E2=
=80=9D may not =E2=80=9Cdistort significant historical events,=E2=80=9D =E2=
=80=9Cinclude a curriculum that teaches identity politics,=E2=80=9D or be b=
ased on theories that systemic racism, sexism, oppression, and privilege ar=
e inherent in American institutions.<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/c=
hapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn1" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref1" role=
=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/=
Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn1" aria-expanded=3D"false"><sup>1</sup></a></p>
</blockquote><div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><d=
iv id=3D"fn1"><p><sup>1</sup>&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://www-chronicle-com.pro=
xy.library.stonybrook.edu/article/the-curricular-cull" data-original-href=
=3D"https://www-chronicle-com.proxy.library.stonybrook.edu/article/the-curr=
icular-cull">=E2=80=9CIs Sociology Being Saved =E2=80=94 or Debased =E2=80=
=94 in Florida?=E2=80=9D</a> <em>Chronicle of Higher Education</em>, March =
16, 2026.</p></div></div></div>
<p>There is much to unpack in this statement. For now, we will leave aside =
the meaning of =E2=80=9Ccultural Marxism.=E2=80=9D<a href=3D"https://nhwils=
on.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn2" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=
=3D"fnref2" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.git=
hub.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn2" aria-expanded=3D"false"><sup>2</s=
up></a> We will also only briefly note that colleges explicitly advertise t=
hemselves as places to =E2=80=9Cpush boundaries, challenge assumptions and =
redefine what=E2=80=99s possible,=E2=80=9D<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.githu=
b.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn3" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref3=
" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/cha=
pters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn3" aria-expanded=3D"false"><sup>3</sup></a> so=
 one would <em>expect</em> young people to <em>want</em> to discuss new and=
 interesting ideas with their family. We will also only glancingly note tha=
t =E2=80=9Cpromot[ing] and preserv[ing] the constitutional republic=E2=80=
=9D is <em>itself</em> an ideological goal, and that pursuing it explicitly=
 could and would bias our understanding of society just like an explicitly =
=E2=80=9Cwoke=E2=80=9D one would.</p>
<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div id=3D"fn2"=
><p><sup>2</sup>&nbsp;I seriously doubt that Rodrigues and his allies are r=
eading Gramsci, Lukacs, or Benjamin, and instead, are simply using =E2=80=
=9Ccultural Marxism=E2=80=9D as a catch-all for ideologies they don=E2=80=
=99t like. See A.J.A. Woods, <em>The Cultural Marxism Conspiracy</em>, Vers=
o, 2026.</p></div><div id=3D"fn3"><p><sup>3</sup>&nbsp;<a href=3D"https://w=
ww.stonybrook.edu/" data-original-href=3D"https://www.stonybrook.edu/">Ston=
y Brook=E2=80=99s Webpage</a>, accessed June 27th, 2026.</p></div></div><p>=
Instead, I want to take aim at what seems to be a foundational assumption o=
f those criticizing sociology: that there is a way to pursue a completely u=
nbiased, objective, neutral study of society. The authors of the Flordia st=
ate law imply, by claiming to cleanse University curriculum of =E2=80=9Cdis=
tortions,=E2=80=9D that there <em>is</em> an objective, true, and indisputa=
ble nature of society and history that is like the things we know about the=
 physical history of the universe, biological dynamics, or the engineering =
of the material environment.</p>
<p>As you will see below, at a very basic level sociology (and the rest of =
the social sciences) do not work this way. This is because they take as the=
ir central object <em>people in society</em>, and since people <em>react</e=
m> to their understandings about the world and the meaning they take from i=
t, they need to be studied in a different way than rocks, planets, and path=
ogens.<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#f=
n4" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref4" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original=
-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn4" aria=
-expanded=3D"false"><sup>4</sup></a> However, this does <em>not</em> mean t=
hat the social sciences and particularly sociology are therefore simply a r=
ealm where we assert opinions and call them facts; instead, it means that w=
e have a wide inventory of careful, systematic ways of understanding the wo=
rld, as well as many key concepts and theoretical traditions, and our job h=
ere in part is to introduce them.</p>
<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div id=3D"fn4"=
><p><sup>4</sup>&nbsp;As noted in <a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/cha=
pters/Ch_1_Imagination.html" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.=
io/chapters/Ch_1_Imagination.html">the previous unit</a>, this is Du Bois=
=E2=80=99 notion of <strong>reflexivity</strong>.</p></div></div></section>
<section id=3D"what-is-a-science" class=3D"level1 page-columns page-full">
<h1>What Is A Science?</h1>
<p>To answer the question =E2=80=9Cwhat kind of science is sociology?=E2=80=
=9D, we need to answer a prior question: just what is a <strong>science</st=
rong>? And to do <em>that</em> we have to take yet another step backwards, =
and, in turn, ask two basic questions about the structure of the world:</p>
<ol type=3D"1">
<li>What kinds of things make up the world?</li>
<li>How do we know what those things are?</li>
</ol>
<p>These are both ultimately philosophical questions. The first is a questi=
on of <strong>ontology</strong>=E2=80=94sometimes also called =E2=80=9Cmeta=
physics=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94and represents our best understanding of not only =
what the smallest, most basic parts of the world are, but also how those pa=
rts combine into all of the things that exist. The second question, meanwhi=
le, is one of <strong>epistemology</strong>; <em>if</em> the world is popul=
ated with certain things, how can we know what they are?</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
>
<p><strong>Ontology</strong> is the study of the basic building blocks of t=
he natural world. <strong>Epistemology</strong> is the study of ways of kno=
wing about and verifying the accuracy of our knowledge about the world.</p>
</div></div><p>Since the emergence of modern science about 500 years ago (d=
epending on how you=E2=80=99re counting; see <span class=3D"citation" data-=
cites=3D"shapinSocialHistoryTruth1994">Shapin (<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.=
github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-shapinSocialHistoryTruth1994" r=
ole=3D"doc-biblioref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chap=
ters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-shapinSocialHistoryTruth1994" aria-expanded=
=3D"false">1994</a>)</span>), a strong consensus has grown around two epist=
emological commitments. The first is that all explanations and descriptions=
 of things in the world=E2=80=94<em>what</em> is there, <em>how</em> those =
things interrelate, and <em>how</em> we know=E2=80=94must be <strong>natura=
l</strong>. Bluntly, this means that scientists aim to explain things in th=
e world as the result of the workings of other observable things in the wor=
ld, and not as the result of the will of one or another deity.</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
>
<p><strong>Naturalistic explanation</strong> is a key aspect of scientific =
epistemology. It dictates that all phenomena subject to scientific descript=
ion and explanation should relate only to processes and things in the world=
, not to supernatural forces.</p>
</div></div><p>The second point of consensus among all forms of science is =
a (more-or-less) <strong>skeptical attitude</strong> towards existing expla=
nations about the world. This means that, rather than accepting a descripti=
on or explanation about how things work in the world just because they come=
 from someone we respect or fear, we instead try to objectively verify that=
 a given process <em>actually</em> works that way. Crucially, this does not=
 mean that <em>anyone</em> is qualified to question scientific findings (si=
nce they often depend on years of experience and training to understand ext=
remely complex processes), but rather that communities of scientists should=
 work as hard as they can to subject all explanations to the most rigorous =
scrutiny they can imagine <span class=3D"citation" data-cites=3D"mertonNorm=
ativeStructureScience1979">(<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/=
Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-mertonNormativeStructureScience1979" role=3D"doc-b=
iblioref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Di=
scipline.html#ref-mertonNormativeStructureScience1979" aria-expanded=3D"fal=
se">Merton 1979</a>)</span>.</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
>
<p>In scientific epistemology, the <strong>skeptical attitude</strong> is a=
 commitment held by scientists to subject potential descriptions and explan=
ations about the world to the most rigorous examination possible to verify =
them.</p>
</div></div><p>As mentioned, commitments to skepticism and naturalism are k=
ey parts of what defines a science. But there are also important tensions a=
nd divergences within science. Three of the most central to sociology are w=
orth addressing in some depth.</p>
<section id=3D"the-problem-of-realism-vs.-empiricism" class=3D"level2 page-=
columns page-full">
<h2 class=3D"anchored" data-anchor-id=3D"the-problem-of-realism-vs.-empiric=
ism">The Problem of Realism vs.&nbsp;Empiricism<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " =
aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" href=3D"https://nhwi=
lson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#the-problem-of-realism-vs.-emp=
iricism" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; margin-left: 0.1875em; padd=
ing-right: 0.1875em; padding-left: 0.1875em;"></a></h2>
<p>First and foremost, a central epistemological tension in science concern=
s the observability of phenomena under study. Most basically, things can be=
 observed directly with our senses=E2=80=94we can see, hear, feel, touch, a=
nd taste what we want to know about. At the level of our raw, physical expe=
rience of the natural world, this gives us a lot of information necessary t=
o survive. For example, I know my coffee cup won=E2=80=99t fall through the=
 table right now. Why? Because, before I put it down, I can verify that the=
 table has a physical property of being =E2=80=9Chard=E2=80=9D by touching =
it, and therefore be (pretty) sure that this will persist as long as the cu=
p is there. Philosophers of science call this epistemological approach <str=
ong>empiricism</strong>.</p>
<p>But there is <em>large</em> class of phenomena=E2=80=94containing most o=
f what sociologists care about=E2=80=94that are not readily accessible to a=
n individual=E2=80=99s direct observation in this way. For instance, think =
of <span class=3D"smallcaps">the market</span>.<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.=
github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn5" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"f=
nref5" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.i=
o/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn5" aria-expanded=3D"false"><sup>5</sup></=
a> Yes, you <em>can</em> go down to Smith Haven Mall, or even just to the f=
ood trucks collected outside the Student Activities Center, and see <em>par=
ts</em> of <span class=3D"smallcaps">the market</span>. But what prices you=
 will pay in either of those places, and, indeed, what will even be availab=
le to you, are actually driven by a world-spanning social structure.<a href=
=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn6" class=3D"=
footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref6" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"http=
s://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn6" aria-expanded=3D"=
false"><sup>6</sup></a> So those two places are <em>a part of</em> <span cl=
ass=3D"smallcaps">the market</span>, but only parts, and there is literally=
 no way to observe the whole thing; instead, scientists can only observe th=
e <em>traces</em> and <em>parts</em> of it that <em>are</em> available to o=
bservation, and then work backwards, asking themselves =E2=80=9Chow would <=
span class=3D"smallcaps">the market</span> have to work for us to see price=
s, the availability of goods, and so on work in this way=E2=80=9D?</p>
<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div id=3D"fn5"=
><p><sup>5</sup>&nbsp;I=E2=80=99m using smallcaps to denote <span class=3D"=
smallcaps">the market</span>, in the sense of the real social structure, an=
d =E2=80=9Cmarkets=E2=80=9D to denote concrete venues for buying and sellin=
g.</p></div><div id=3D"fn6"><p><sup>6</sup>&nbsp;For the food trucks, this =
is the =E2=80=9Cglobal commodities market=E2=80=9D for foodstuffs and other=
 materials, which indirectly set the prices the vendors pay to their wholes=
alers, and which thus determines whether they can profitably offer you a ta=
sty, tasty burrito. If wholesale prices become too high, they must either g=
ive you less of the best parts of the burrito (obviously the pulled pork) f=
or the same price=E2=80=94=E2=80=9Cshrinkflation=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94or substi=
tute something less expensive.</p></div></div><p>In this case, <span class=
=3D"smallcaps">the market</span> <em>is</em> real and a part of the world, =
since it has effects we can observe and measure, and philosophers of scienc=
e therefore call the process of reasoning backwards from those <em>observab=
le effects</em> to the processes that cause them <strong>realism</strong>.<=
/p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
 style=3D"margin-top: 73.4297px;">
<p><strong>Realism</strong> is the scientific approach of reasoning backwar=
ds from observable traces of a phenomenon to assess its structure and gener=
al effects in the world.</p>
</div></div><p>Now, if you=E2=80=99re thinking that there is a <em>large</e=
m> gulf between these two positions, you=E2=80=99re right! A key way that s=
cientists bridge the =E2=80=9Cdirectly observable=E2=80=9D with =E2=80=9Cre=
al but unobservable processes=E2=80=9D is through <strong>instrumentation</=
strong>, or reliable machines and procedures that allow them to collect mea=
surements that are only indirectly accessible.</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
 style=3D"margin-top: 71.0625px;">
<p>Scientists use <strong>instrumentation</strong> to gather more precise m=
easurements and observations about the operation of phenomena that are not =
ordinarily available to human senses.</p>
</div></div><p>Continuing with the example of <span class=3D"smallcaps">the=
 market</span> above, let=E2=80=99s say that economists and economic sociol=
ogists want to know whether prices are generally going up or down for consu=
mers. In the United States, we have a government agency, the <a href=3D"htt=
ps://www.bls.gov/cpi/questions-and-answers.htm" data-original-href=3D"https=
://www.bls.gov/cpi/questions-and-answers.htm">Bureau of Labor Statistics</a=
> that conducts a survey called the =E2=80=9CConsumer Price Index=E2=80=9D,=
 which</p>
<blockquote class=3D"blockquote">
<p>=E2=80=A6is a measure of the average change over time in the prices paid=
 by consumers for a representative basket of consumer goods and services. T=
he CPI measures inflation as experienced by consumers in their day-to-day l=
iving expenses.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote class=3D"blockquote">
<p>The CPI represents all goods and services purchased for consumption by t=
he reference population. BLS has classified all expenditure items into more=
 than 200 categories, arranged into eight major groups (food and beverages,=
 housing, apparel, transportation, medical care, recreation, education and =
communication, and other goods and services). Included within these major g=
roups are various government-charged user fees, such as water and sewerage =
charges, auto registration fees, and vehicle tolls.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The BLS works extremely hard to construct a sample that <em>accurately</=
em> measures the =E2=80=9Creal=E2=80=9D CPI, but the point for our example =
here is that they are seeking to measure a <em>general property of the cons=
umer goods market in the US</em>, and not any individual, particular price.=
 In sum, you could not obtain this description of the behavior of <span cla=
ss=3D"smallcaps">the market</span> in the US without the instrument of this=
 survey, or something like it.<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapter=
s/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn7" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref7" role=3D"do=
c-noteref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_D=
iscipline.html#fn7" aria-expanded=3D"false"><sup>7</sup></a></p>
<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div id=3D"fn7"=
><p><sup>7</sup>&nbsp;Incidentally, most =E2=80=9Chard=E2=80=9D sciences wo=
rk like this too. Take the <a href=3D"https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/=
04/eight-months-early-and-under-budget-the-roman-telescope-is-ready-to-laun=
ch/" data-original-href=3D"https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/04/eight-mo=
nths-early-and-under-budget-the-roman-telescope-is-ready-to-launch/">Nancy =
Grace Roman Space Telescope</a>, soon to be launched. It works by scanning =
the infrared spectrum of light, which you can=E2=80=99t see with your eyes,=
 but which is well-enough verified and understood that it has widespread ap=
plications today.</p></div></div></section>
<section id=3D"the-problem-of-emergence-vs.-reduction" class=3D"level2 page=
-columns page-full">
<h2 class=3D"anchored" data-anchor-id=3D"the-problem-of-emergence-vs.-reduc=
tion">The Problem of Emergence vs.&nbsp;Reduction<a class=3D"anchorjs-link =
" aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" href=3D"https://nh=
wilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#the-problem-of-emergence-vs.=
-reduction" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; margin-left: 0.1875em; p=
adding-right: 0.1875em; padding-left: 0.1875em;"></a></h2>
<p>The tension between realism and empiricism is heavily epistemological, b=
ut another tension in science involves ontology, and can be understood thro=
ugh instrumentation. Most scientific instruments are built (and validated) =
using assumptions about what kinds of objects they are searching for. To co=
ntinue with our example above of <span class=3D"smallcaps">the market</span=
>, if we want to know its properties, it is probably not a good idea to int=
erview a single consumer and ask about their feelings regarding prices.<a h=
ref=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn8" class=
=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref8" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"=
https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn8" aria-expanded=
=3D"false"><sup>8</sup></a> Yet at the same time, the behavior and properti=
es of <span class=3D"smallcaps">the market</span> are driven by the actions=
 of innumerable people acting (according to classical economics)<a href=3D"=
https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn9" class=3D"foot=
note-ref" id=3D"fnref9" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"https://=
nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn9" aria-expanded=3D"fals=
e"><sup>9</sup></a> simply trying to maximize their own local interests.</p=
>
<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div id=3D"fn8"=
><p><sup>8</sup>&nbsp;Scientists do, however, survey the <em>collective</em=
> sentiments of populations about the market. The most famous is done by th=
e <a href=3D"https://www.sca.isr.umich.edu/" data-original-href=3D"https://=
www.sca.isr.umich.edu/">University of Michigan</a>.</p></div><div id=3D"fn9=
"><p><sup>9</sup>&nbsp;The classical statement of this perspective of the =
=E2=80=9Cinvisible hand,=E2=80=9D which vastly oversimplifies the perspecti=
ve of contemporary economics, is from <span class=3D"citation" data-cites=
=3D"smithWealthNations1976">Smith (<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/ch=
apters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-smithWealthNations1976" role=3D"doc-biblior=
ef" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipli=
ne.html#ref-smithWealthNations1976" aria-expanded=3D"false">1976</a>)</span=
>.</p></div><div id=3D"fn10"><p><sup>10</sup>&nbsp;And indeed, how could we=
? My own approach is pragmatic=E2=80=94different ontological perspectives a=
re useful to do different things, and the question is what each allows and =
forbids, not which one is correct for all time. See <span class=3D"citation=
" data-cites=3D"hackingRepresentingInterveningIntroductory1983">Hacking (<a=
 href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-hacki=
ngRepresentingInterveningIntroductory1983" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-orig=
inal-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-h=
ackingRepresentingInterveningIntroductory1983" aria-expanded=3D"false">1983=
</a>)</span>.</p></div></div><p>But which of these two perspectives=E2=80=
=94collective or individual=E2=80=94shows the =E2=80=9Ctrue=E2=80=9D nature=
 of <span class=3D"smallcaps">the market</span>? This is not a philosophy c=
lass, so thankfully, we don=E2=80=99t have to solve this ontological questi=
on.<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn10=
" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref10" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-=
href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn10" aria=
-expanded=3D"false"><sup>10</sup></a> Instead, it reveals that different sc=
ientific disciplines are at least partly defined by which ontological level=
 they assume is fundamental to their studies.</p>
<p>At one extreme, some disciplines try to <strong>analytically reduce</str=
ong> their objects of study to those that they purport to be the most funda=
mental in the universe. Thus, the last time I checked, physics recognizes s=
eventeen <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elementary_particle" data=
-original-href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elementary_particle">fundam=
ental particles</a>, and literally everything everywhere is composed of the=
m.<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn11"=
 class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref11" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-h=
ref=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn11" aria-=
expanded=3D"false"><sup>11</sup></a></p>
<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div id=3D"fn11=
"><p><sup>11</sup>&nbsp;Fascinatingly, while this is the most reductive ont=
ology that has achieved widespread scientific consensus, it has deficiences=
, and there are rumblings of even <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Physics_beyond_the_Standard_Model" data-original-href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Physics_beyond_the_Standard_Model">further reductions</a>.</p><=
/div><div class=3D"">
<p><strong>Analytic reduction</strong> is the assertion that complex phenom=
ena are actually collections of constituent parts, and therefore best studi=
ed in terms of those smaller components.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Analytic reduction can be extremely powerful and it has revealed some of=
 the most fundamental mechanics of the world around us that we know of. But=
 also think of the phenomena in the world that analytic reduction makes it =
<em>harder</em> to study. Let=E2=80=99s go back to <span class=3D"smallcaps=
">the market</span>, for instance. <em>If</em> you <del>were insane and</de=
l> wanted to study <span class=3D"smallcaps">the market</span> in terms of =
quantum physics, you would <em>at least</em> have to make the following ste=
ps:</p>
<ol type=3D"1">
<li>Explain how quantum particles become atoms.</li>
<li>Explain how atoms become molecules.</li>
<li>Explain how inorganic molecules become organisms.</li>
<li>Explain how organisms=E2=80=99 neural functions become sufficiently com=
plex to attain conscious and intentionality.</li>
<li>Explain how consciousnesses form collective abstract representations.</=
li>
<li>Explain how those representations evolve and grow more complex to becom=
e the modern form of <span class=3D"smallcaps">the market</span>.</li>
</ol>
<p>To my knowledge,<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Disc=
ipline.html#fn12" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref12" role=3D"doc-noteref=
" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline=
.html#fn12" aria-expanded=3D"false"><sup>12</sup></a> only one of these ste=
ps (step 2) is well-understood, and if you tried to move across all 6, you =
would easily exhaust your own life trying to just explain <em>one</em> comp=
lex phenomenon.</p>
<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div id=3D"fn12=
"><p><sup>12</sup>&nbsp;Those of you who know more than me can tell me whet=
her the other steps have been decisively accounted for.</p></div></div><p>B=
y consequence of the difficulty of working all the way back up from purport=
edly fundamental elements to complex phenomena, many sciences focus on the =
properties that <strong>emerge</strong> at a given level of complexity. In =
other words, for <span class=3D"smallcaps">the market</span>, you don=E2=80=
=99t need to know the quantum-physical properties of the gold being sold in=
 (one part of) it, because they are irrelevant to what you are trying to ex=
plain=E2=80=94say, the availability of gold at a certain price and at a cer=
tain time. Likewise, a clinician who suspects one of their patients is subj=
ect to <a href=3D"https://www.cdc.gov/intimate-partner-violence/about/index=
.html" data-original-href=3D"https://www.cdc.gov/intimate-partner-violence/=
about/index.html">intimate partner violence</a> is unlikely to be concerned=
 by the organic chemistry of their patients=E2=80=99 injuries; instead, the=
y will likely focus on the psychological and social processes at work.</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
 style=3D"margin-top: 2.10156px;">
<p><strong>Emergence</strong> is the unfolding of new properties and higher=
 levels of ontological complexity, which can be apprehended and studied wit=
hout reference to subsidiary ontological levels.</p>
</div><div id=3D"fn13"><p><sup>13</sup>&nbsp;For example, <em>sociological<=
/em> social psychology tends to criticize <em>psychological</em> social psy=
chology as having an impoverished account of the emergent properties of soc=
ial structure and culture, whereas in the opposite direction, psychologists=
 see sociologists as having woefully imprecise concepts of =E2=80=9Cthe sel=
f,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cpersonality,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9Cmotivation,=E2=80=9D an=
d so on=E2=80=94which is to say, they have insufficiently analytically redu=
ced their concepts to be useful.</p></div></div><p>Throughout this discussi=
on, you=E2=80=99ll notice that I have not once asserted that any particular=
 ontological level is =E2=80=9Cright,=E2=80=9D nor that reduction or emerge=
nce is always the best approach. But I also want to emphasize that the boun=
daries among areas of research are not at all immutable. Indeed, some of th=
e most interesting areas of science today are at the boundaries between red=
uction and emergence, and protective =E2=80=9Cborder skirmishes=E2=80=9D be=
tween disciplines tend to be fought over them.<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.g=
ithub.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn13" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"f=
nref13" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.=
io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn13" aria-expanded=3D"false"><sup>13</sup=
></a> For instance, a great deal of work is being done trying to understand=
 the intersection between the biochemical processes in the brain and our se=
nse of ourselves as conscious beings. And an emergent field of study is ask=
ing whether LLMs=E2=80=94trained on our collective discursive output=E2=80=
=94can be studied directly as repositories of social dynamics not evident i=
n any given piece of discourse. Instead, the ontological levels assumed by =
given disciplines act like centers of gravity around which most of the work=
 considered to be =E2=80=9Cin=E2=80=9D that discipline orbits.</p>
</section>
<section id=3D"the-problem-of-subjectivity-intersubjectivity-and-objectivit=
y" class=3D"level2 page-columns page-full">
<h2 class=3D"anchored" data-anchor-id=3D"the-problem-of-subjectivity-inters=
ubjectivity-and-objectivity">The Problem of Subjectivity, Intersubjectivity=
, and Objectivity<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anc=
horjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_D=
iscipline.html#the-problem-of-subjectivity-intersubjectivity-and-objectivit=
y" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; margin-left: 0.1875em; padding-ri=
ght: 0.1875em; padding-left: 0.1875em;"></a></h2>
<p>A final tension in science worth discussing involves the source of obser=
vations about the world=E2=80=94people themselves. People are both ontologi=
cal and epistemological problems for science.</p>
<p>Ontologically, they represent something that is maddeningly hard to obse=
rve, which is interior self-conscious states and intentional motivations ab=
out the future. This is usually summarized as their <strong>subjective</str=
ong>. Worse still, people are also capable of <em>coordination</em>, which =
is to say they can exchange meaning<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/ch=
apters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn14" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref14" rol=
e=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters=
/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn14" aria-expanded=3D"false"><sup>14</sup></a> with =
one another to align themselves into regular patterns, or even to <em>chang=
e</em> the state of the world through collective action to bring about some=
thing they imagine to be possible. Scientists usually call this kind of exc=
hange and coordination <strong>intersubjective</strong>. And if even <em>th=
at</em> were not complex enough, there are also sets of social and material=
 forces working on whole populations, the planet itself, and physical proce=
sses that lie beyond the reach of even the most elaborate coordinated actio=
n we have ever observed. It is these phenomena we usually call <strong>obje=
ctive.</strong></p>
<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div id=3D"fn14=
"><p><sup>14</sup>&nbsp;The subject of <a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.i=
o/chapters/Ch_4_Meaning.html" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github=
.io/chapters/Ch_4_Meaning.html">unit 4</a>!</p></div><div class=3D"">
<p>A <strong>subjective</strong> ontology emphasizes people=E2=80=99s inter=
ior states of mind, beliefs, and intentions about the world around them. An=
 <strong>intersubjective</strong> ontology concentrates on the shared belie=
fs, goals, and intentions held by groups of people coordinating their actio=
n. And an <strong>objective</strong> ontology emphasizes how non-human mate=
rial and large-scale social forces affect society and individual people.</p=
>
</div></div>
<p>Each of these three ontological levels also carries epistemological chal=
lenges for scientists. Scientists usually try to avoid purely <strong>subje=
ctivist</strong> interpretations, which is to say, those than depend on an =
individual=E2=80=99s perspective=E2=80=94or those belonging to one social c=
ategory and not another=E2=80=94and aim to gather knowledge that transcends=
 such narrow perspectives (or, at least, can be translated across perspecti=
ves).</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
>
<p>A <strong>subjectivist</strong> epistemology focuses on people=E2=80=99s=
 internal mental states and how they influence people=E2=80=99s actions and=
 beliefs about the world.</p>
</div></div><p>However, it is also the case that one=E2=80=99s subjectivity=
, when used reflexively, can be a key mode of gathering knowledge about the=
 world. To take a trivial example, it is well-known that some people experi=
ence <a href=3D"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6233698/" data-ori=
ginal-href=3D"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6233698/">white coat=
 hypertension</a> where, because they are nervous to have their blood press=
ure taken in a hospital setting, their blood pressure measures higher than =
it would otherwise. Depending on what you want to know, the patient=E2=80=
=99s subjectivity=E2=80=94that is, their sense of themselves and the situat=
ion given their social position in a given setting=E2=80=94is either a sour=
ce of scientific bias (about their =E2=80=9Ctrue=E2=80=9D blood pressure) o=
r a phenomenon worth directly studying itself.</p>
<p>There are also <strong>intersubjectivist</strong> epistemological tensio=
ns in science. On the one hand, as noted above, the apparatus of modern sci=
entific instrumentation means that to be able to meaningfully contribute to=
 science often takes <em>years</em> of specialized training, leaving only a=
 tiny proportion of the overall population qualified to skeptically examine=
 scientific claims.<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Disc=
ipline.html#fn15" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref15" role=3D"doc-noteref=
" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline=
.html#fn15" aria-expanded=3D"false"><sup>15</sup></a> At the same time, to =
the extent that intersubjective scientific communities become locked into a=
 single collective perspective, the very boundaries of expertise can also b=
ecome blinders, and crucial phenomena in the world can be left under- or un=
studied.</p>
<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div id=3D"fn15=
"><p><sup>15</sup>&nbsp;I have no scientifically-sound idea whether the bet=
ter avenue for pursuing controlled nuclear fusion power is a <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokamak" data-original-href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Tokamak">Tokamak Reactor</a> or a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Stellarator" data-original-href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Stellarator">Stellarator</a>. Do you?</p></div><div class=3D"">
<p>An <strong>intersubjectivist</strong> epistemology emphasizes how scient=
ific perspectives, topics of inquiry, and interpretations are shaped by dis=
ciplinary experts in communication with one another.</p>
</div></div>
<p>Partly in response to the threats of subjectivist and intersubjectivist =
bias, some scientists harken for an <strong>objectivist</strong> epistemolo=
gy. Here, the best knowledge is that cleansed of the corruptions of individ=
ual moral or political commitments biasing the conduct of research, and fro=
m being =E2=80=9Csteered=E2=80=9D towards one or another topic or conclusio=
n by a disciplinary community. At a minimum, these scientists believe, this=
 should mean that scientists commit to reporting their findings even if the=
y show the opposite of what they hoped, or that scientific communities shou=
ld protect or even reward conducting work on unpopular or controversial top=
ics. At maximum, however, objectivist epistemology seeks a =E2=80=9Cview fr=
om nowhere=E2=80=9D <span class=3D"citation" data-cites=3D"nagelViewNowhere=
1989">(<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#=
ref-nagelViewNowhere1989" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-original-href=3D"http=
s://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-nagelViewNowhere19=
89" aria-expanded=3D"false">Nagel 1989</a>)</span>, completely detached and=
 independent from any social influence.</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
>
<p>An <strong>objectivist</strong> epistemology seeks to eliminate subjecti=
ve and intersubjective bias from inquiry, and ultimately achieve a totally =
independent view of reality.</p>
</div></div><p>One tension in this objectivist epistemology should be obvio=
us: there is <em>no</em> such thing as a position outside of society from w=
hich to study it, and plenty of social phenomena are constituted depending =
on concrete social positions only accessible to certain scientists. To this=
 objection, defenders typically respond that instead of a purely objective =
ontological view, we should instead <em>aspire</em> to being as objective a=
s we can, and that a crucial step in that direction is to deliberately admi=
t and reward those with views outside of the scientific mainstream into the=
 intersubjective conversation.</p>
</section>
</section>
<section id=3D"key-concepts-and-theories-in-contemporary-sociology" class=
=3D"level1 page-columns page-full">
<h1>Key Concepts and Theories in Contemporary Sociology</h1>
<p>So now we have dined on some philosophy=E2=80=94hopefully without chokin=
g!=E2=80=94and are ready for another course, this time at the sociological =
buffet. Here, my goal is to serve you a taste of what we will savor through=
 the rest of the course: concepts that we will see over and over. I also ho=
pe you=E2=80=99ll see how their flavors are all the richer for the philosop=
hical background about science that we have just covered!<a href=3D"https:/=
/nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn16" class=3D"footnote-r=
ef" id=3D"fnref16" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwil=
son.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn16" aria-expanded=3D"false"><=
sup>16</sup></a></p>
<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div id=3D"fn16=
"><p><sup>16</sup>&nbsp;Okay, okay, I=E2=80=99ll stop beating the =E2=80=9C=
food=E2=80=9D metaphor to death!</p></div></div><section id=3D"key-concepts=
" class=3D"level2 page-columns page-full">
<h2 class=3D"anchored" data-anchor-id=3D"key-concepts">Key Concepts<a class=
=3D"anchorjs-link " aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" =
href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#key-concep=
ts" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; margin-left: 0.1875em; padding-r=
ight: 0.1875em; padding-left: 0.1875em;"></a></h2>
<section id=3D"social-construction" class=3D"level3 page-columns page-full"=
>
<h3 class=3D"anchored" data-anchor-id=3D"social-construction">Social Constr=
uction<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=
=3D"=EE=A7=8B" href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.=
html#social-construction" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; margin-lef=
t: 0.1875em; padding-right: 0.1875em; padding-left: 0.1875em;"></a></h3>
<p>One of the simplest and most direct, but also most notorious, concepts i=
n sociology is <strong>social construction</strong>. If you have ever playe=
d any kind of game, engaged in make-believe with a young child, read fictio=
n, or traveled in an unfamiliar culture, you have encountered social constr=
uctions and been aware of doing so. But <em>also</em>, if you ever used a b=
ank, been in love, celebrated a birthday, or spoken to another person, you=
=E2=80=99ve also encountered a social construction, but perhaps with less a=
wareness. Simply put, the concept means that a phenomenon in the world exis=
ts because of shared belief in its existence, and people behaving as though=
 it exists.</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
>
<p><strong>Social construction</strong> is when a phenomenon is constituted=
 by shared belief in its existence.</p>
</div><div id=3D"fn17"><p><sup>17</sup>&nbsp;For a lovely exposition of a s=
ophisticated constructionist view, see <span class=3D"citation" data-cites=
=3D"hackingHistoricalOntology2004">Hacking (<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.git=
hub.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-hackingHistoricalOntology2004" rol=
e=3D"doc-biblioref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapte=
rs/Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-hackingHistoricalOntology2004" aria-expanded=3D=
"false">2004</a>)</span>.</p></div><div id=3D"fn18"><div class=3D"quarto-fi=
gure quarto-figure-center"><sup>18</sup>&nbsp;
<figure class=3D"figure">
<p><img src=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/images/2_Burrito.jpeg" class=3D"i=
mg-fluid figure-img"></p>
<figcaption>A Burrito, because I realized I haven=E2=80=99t used an image i=
n this unit (<a href=3D"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burrito.JPG=
" data-original-href=3D"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burrito.JPG=
">Source</a>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
</div></div><p>Crucially, this does not mean that individual people can sim=
ply make up whatever they want or that nothing in the world is =E2=80=9Crea=
l.=E2=80=9D<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.h=
tml#fn17" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fnref17" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-o=
riginal-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn=
17" aria-expanded=3D"false"><sup>17</sup></a> Instead, it means that most o=
f what we treat as real and self-evident for the purposes of getting throug=
h our everyday lives is an unproblematic <em>composite</em> of objective, m=
aterial matter and the meanings we attach to it. We can (usually) treat the=
 currency we hold in our hands as =E2=80=9Cgood=E2=80=9D to exchange for so=
mething else, and don=E2=80=99t think twice about the fact that (US) curren=
cy is actually <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fiat_money" data-or=
iginal-href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fiat_money">fiat money</a>, an=
d ultimately depends on global trust in the soundness of the US economy and=
 treasury. And if we are using a credit card or our phones, we (usually) do=
n=E2=80=99t even think about the fact that the =E2=80=9Cdollars=E2=80=9D we=
 are spending are in fact simply electronic entries on various ledgers bein=
g changed, or that <a href=3D"https://www.federalreserve.gov/releases/h6/cu=
rrent/default.htm" data-original-href=3D"https://www.federalreserve.gov/rel=
eases/h6/current/default.htm">currency in circulation</a> is a very differe=
nt thing (much smaller) than the <a href=3D"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/ser=
ies/M2SL" data-original-href=3D"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/M2SL">to=
tal money supply</a>. We just buy that tasty, tasty burrito.<a href=3D"http=
s://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn18" class=3D"footnot=
e-ref" id=3D"fnref18" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"https://nh=
wilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn18" aria-expanded=3D"false=
"><sup>18</sup></a></p>
</section>
<section id=3D"micro-and-macro" class=3D"level3 page-columns page-full">
<h3 class=3D"anchored" data-anchor-id=3D"micro-and-macro">Micro and Macro<a=
 class=3D"anchorjs-link " aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=
=A7=8B" href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#mi=
cro-and-macro" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; margin-left: 0.1875em=
; padding-right: 0.1875em; padding-left: 0.1875em;"></a></h3>
<p>Sociology as a whole is usually concerned with intersubjective phenomena=
, but that intersubjectivity can take place at (at least) two very differen=
t scales. One is at the level of face-to-face interaction, and, indeed, a g=
reat deal of communication takes place in these venues: we shake hands, eat=
 together, smile or frown at one another, laugh, cry, yell, fight, love, an=
d so on. When we are talking like this about our interactions with the imme=
diately-present world and interactions within a given social setting, socio=
logists describe this as the <strong>micro</strong> level of analysis.</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
>
<p><strong>Micro-level</strong> analysis focuses on face-to-face interactio=
ns with other people and interactions with the immediately present world.</=
p>
</div></div><p>But as I=E2=80=99ve alluded to already, many phenomena socio=
logists care about exist at a higher level than just face-to-face interacti=
ons. Think about =E2=80=9CStony Brook University,=E2=80=9D for instance. It=
=E2=80=99s not <em>just</em> the buildings, faculty, students, and staff th=
at make it up; instead, it is an =E2=80=9Corganization=E2=80=9D that enforc=
es durable patterns, and allows us to operate at a surprisingly-efficient l=
evel of coordination. Put differently, even if <em>one</em> section of SOC =
105 was cancelled, SBU would continue to be a university, but at the same t=
ime, it could not be a university without having <em>some kind of</em> clas=
ses. Taken together, a huge amount of the apparatus of modern life works li=
ke this, and collectively, sociologists call this level of analysis <strong=
>macro</strong>.</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
>
<p><strong>Macro-level</strong> analysis focuses on scales of phenomena bey=
ond the level of face-to-face, direct interaction with the world.</p>
</div></div><p>One key area of sociological research today is how these two=
 levels intersect <span class=3D"citation" data-cites=3D"martinSocialStruct=
ures2009">(<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.h=
tml#ref-martinSocialStructures2009" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-original-hr=
ef=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-martinSo=
cialStructures2009" aria-expanded=3D"false">Martin 2009</a>)</span>, and wh=
ether or not they represent different ontological levels of social life <sp=
an class=3D"citation" data-cites=3D"hedstromDissectingSocialPrinciples2005"=
>(<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-h=
edstromDissectingSocialPrinciples2005" role=3D"doc-biblioref" data-original=
-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#ref-hedst=
romDissectingSocialPrinciples2005" aria-expanded=3D"false">Hedstrom 2005</a=
>)</span>.</p>
</section>
<section id=3D"agency-and-structure" class=3D"level3 page-columns page-full=
">
<h3 class=3D"anchored" data-anchor-id=3D"agency-and-structure">Agency and S=
tructure<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " aria-label=3D"Anchor" data-anchorjs-ico=
n=3D"=EE=A7=8B" href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline=
.html#agency-and-structure" style=3D"font: 1em / 1 anchorjs-icons; margin-l=
eft: 0.1875em; padding-right: 0.1875em; padding-left: 0.1875em;"></a></h3>
<p>At the level of our personal lives, we all have choices. And when we org=
anize collectively, those groups, too, can choose one course of action or a=
nother. In turn, these choices can make a difference in our individual live=
s, or even on the fates of whole organizations or societies. Sociologists c=
all this process of making a difference in the world <strong>agency</strong=
> and the entities doing so <strong>agents</strong>.</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
>
<p><strong>Agency</strong> is the capacity of some entity to make a differe=
nce in the world.</p>
</div></div><p>Meanwhile, much of the social world also appears to us as co=
nstraint=E2=80=94it seems that our lots are set by factors outside of our c=
ontrol. For instance, it would nonsense in contemporary society for you to =
judge someone by saying =E2=80=9Cthey should have picked their parents bett=
er!=E2=80=9D This is because, of course, who we are born to (and, sociologi=
cally, the social class, national origin, race, and so on) is not chosen by=
 us, but rather something we encounter as a given, and then must make our c=
hoices within that framework. We call these kinds of factors in the world <=
strong>structures</strong> and usually thing of them as determining individ=
ual or collective social outcomes.</p>

<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div class=3D""=
>
<p><strong>Structures</strong> are patterned social phenomena that dtermien=
 individual or collective outcomes.</p>
</div></div></section>
<section id=3D"social-order-and-social-change" class=3D"level3 page-columns=
 page-full">
<h3 class=3D"anchored" data-anchor-id=3D"social-order-and-social-change">So=
cial Order and Social Change<a class=3D"anchorjs-link " aria-label=3D"Ancho=
r" data-anchorjs-icon=3D"=EE=A7=8B" href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.io/chap=
ters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#social-order-and-social-change" style=3D"font: 1e=
m / 1 anchorjs-icons; margin-left: 0.1875em; padding-right: 0.1875em; paddi=
ng-left: 0.1875em;"></a></h3>
<p>Finally, when considering social structures over time, sociologists rema=
rk upon two main dynamics. First, and perhaps surprisingly, <strong>social =
order</strong> seems generally resilient, or at least resistant, to total d=
estruction. No matter whether it is a war, natural disaster, or other calam=
ity, <em>some</em> form of patterned collective activity based on a shared =
understanding of the situation seems to emerge.</p>
<p>That being said, specific social structures sometimes change, and someti=
mes they do so quite rapidly. For instance, within <em>your</em> lifetimes,=
 the cheap, easily available technology has emerged and diffused which allo=
ws nearly instantaneous global communication.<a href=3D"https://nhwilson.gi=
thub.io/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn19" class=3D"footnote-ref" id=3D"fn=
ref19" role=3D"doc-noteref" data-original-href=3D"https://nhwilson.github.i=
o/chapters/Ch_2_Discipline.html#fn19" aria-expanded=3D"false"><sup>19</sup>=
</a> This has massively changed how we interact with one another, above all=
, pushing that interaction into new digital spaces.</p>
<div class=3D"no-row-height column-margin column-container"><div id=3D"fn19=
"><p><sup>19</sup>&nbsp;This is, of course, the smart phone.</p></div></div=
><p>This rapid technological change is just one more feature of the long-te=
rm rise to dominance of capitalism as the key mode of organizing our econom=
ic lives. After all, the rise and diffusion of smartphones, and the spread =
of the internet as more than simply <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/ARPANET" data-original-href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ARPANET">an =
investment by the military</a>, required the ability to produce, market and=
 sell phones and internet services as <strong>commodities</strong> at a pro=
fit. (The spread of capitalism has had numerous other effects which we will=
 trace throughout the course.)</p>
<p>A second, and no less important, world-historical change is loosely rela=
ted to the emergence of capitalism, and involves the use of calculation to =
think about an increasing number of aspects of our lives. This process, <st=
rong>rationalization</strong>, entails <em>both</em> the subjection of acti=
on to means-ends efficiency logic (in which case it is called <strong>forma=
l rationalization</strong>) <em>and</em> the articulation of explicit purpo=
ses for action in more and more domains of life (where it is called <strong=
>substantive rationalization</strong>).</p>
</section>
</section>
</section>
